PMAT-535 — a branch names the ticket its work claims
====================================================

### 1. the defect, as it actually happened
PR #532 branch:  PMAT-520-book-v1.29.0
PR #532 merge:   b4719737775ab1ebdb493e019affd0230c994ed8
its trailer:     PMAT-531
and PMAT-520, which SHIPPED in 1.29.0, carries both labels on main today:
  roadmap.yaml:3655  - release:v1.29.0
  roadmap.yaml:3656  - release:v1.30.0

### 2. why the arm reads the line with sed and not with git's trailer parser
The SAME commit, asked two ways:
  git's own parser  %(trailers:key=Pmat-Ticket,valueonly): (nothing)
  sed -n 's/^Pmat-Ticket:...//p'                         : PMAT-531 
git's parser reads the LAST PARAGRAPH only, and this squash message ends
with bullet paragraphs. An arm built on it would have read 'this branch
claims nothing', taken the skip, and let PR #532 through — the very push
this arm exists to refuse.

### 3. the correction: what CB-2113 and the commit-msg hook ACTUALLY do
The first version of this arm said both 'match the LINE wherever it
appears'. Three review lanes refuted it. Measured:

pmat's CB-2113 — src/services/commit_traceability/mod.rs:
  275:            "--format=%H%x1f%s%x1f%(trailers:key=Pmat-Ticket,valueonly,separator=%x2c)",
  320:            "--format=%H%x1f%s%x1f%(trailers:key=Pmat-Ticket,valueonly,separator=%x2c)",
  -> it asks GIT. So CB-2113 saw nothing on b4719737 either.

.git/hooks/commit-msg (generated by pmat hooks install --strict):
  PATTERN='PMAT-[0-9]+|#[0-9]+'
  STRICT=1
  trailer=$(git interpret-trailers --parse < "$MSG_FILE" 2>/dev/null | grep -iE '^Pmat-Ticket:' | head -1 | sed 's/^[^:]*:[[:space:]]*//')
  if [ -n "$trailer" ]; then
      exit 0
  fi
  # Comment lines are not part of the message.
  if grep -vE '^[[:space:]]*#' "$MSG_FILE" | grep -qE "$PATTERN"; then
      exit 0
  fi
  -> git's parser first; on a miss it falls back to grep -qE over the whole
     message, so ANY 'PMAT-[0-9]+|#[0-9]+' anywhere satisfies it. That is
     how every multi-paragraph commit in this session passed.

Reading the LINE is therefore strictly MORE permissive than either tool:
this arm can refuse only a branch whose commits demonstrably name some
other ticket, never one those two consider unlabelled.

